#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wiki-testimony — the operator testimony veracity ledger: what he said, what turned out to be so, and what that predicts.

    bin/wiki-testimony                    the standing state
    bin/wiki-testimony score              the trust score, global and by class
    bin/wiki-testimony assess --class date --confidence certain
    bin/wiki-testimony profile            what and how he gets things wrong
    bin/wiki-testimony record ...         enter a claim
    bin/wiki-testimony adjudicate t004 --outcome refuted ...

WHAT THIS IS

`CLAUDE.md`'s CLOSE operation already says the right thing: an operator answer
is **T0 first-person testimony, not proof**, and where it can be checked against
`raw/` it must be. The wiki has been doing that for months. What it has never
done is *keep the results*. Each check lands as a blockquote on one page —
GAP CLOSED, CONTRADICTION, CORROBORATION ATTEMPTED — and then it is over. Page
41 has no idea that the same person's date claims came back eight weeks early on
page 12, so the next answer is weighed exactly as credulously as the first.

This is the missing half of CLOSE: the place the result of the check is
recorded, so that a hundred scattered adjudications become one instrument
pointed at the next unproven thing he says.

TWO NUMBERS, BECAUSE ONE WOULD BE A LIE

A single "trust score" collapses two independent facts that behave differently
and predict differently, and the collapse is the reason most credibility scores
are useless:

  VERACITY     How often he turns out to be right, weighted by how specific and
               load-bearing the claim was. This is the score in the ordinary
               sense: points gained, points lost.

  CALIBRATION  Whether his own stated confidence tracks that accuracy. A person
               right 70% of the time who says "I think, not sure" on exactly the
               30% is *fully reliable* — you just have to read the hedge. A
               person right 90% of the time who says "definitely" on all of it
               is more accurate and less usable, because nothing in the delivery
               tells you which tenth to distrust.

Veracity says how much to believe him. Calibration says whether to believe his
confidence. Only the second one makes an UNPROVEN claim assessable, which is the
entire point of the exercise — you cannot check the unproven claim, so all you
have is the class it belongs to and the confidence it arrived with.

THE STRATIFICATION IS THE PRODUCT

"Dan is 74% reliable" is not usable by anything. The findings that are usable
look like: *date claims about events more than ten years back come back early
94% of the time, by a median of eight weeks* — because that is a correction you
can actually apply to the next date he gives. So every testimony is filed under
a CLAIM CLASS (what kind of proposition it is), and every failed one under a
FAILURE MODE (how it broke) and a DIRECTION (which way it broke). `profile`
reads those three columns; `assess` turns them into a prior.

SMALL SAMPLES ARE NAMED AS SUCH, ALWAYS

The corpus has tens of adjudications, not thousands, and several classes will
sit at n=1 for a long time. So: every rate carries its n, class rates are
shrunk toward the global rate with a pseudocount, and a Wilson interval is
printed rather than a bare percentage. A class under MIN_N is refused as a
prior outright — `assess` falls back to the global figure and says it did.
This is the same discipline `bin/intake` applies to a mean over 10 of 13
events, for the same reason: a figure that cannot state its own denominator
gets believed as if it had one.

WHAT SCORES NOTHING, DELIBERATELY

  unfalsifiable    Checked, and the corpus cannot settle it. Scores ZERO, never
                   negative. Punishing him for the archive's gaps would make the
                   number a measure of the corpus rather than of the man, and
                   would make honest recording of a hard claim costly.
  unadjudicated    Not yet checked. Excluded from every statistic; it is the
                   queue, not the record.

WHOSE CLAIM IT IS

`subject: self` is a claim about his own conduct, states or history.
`subject: other` is a claim about somebody else. Both are scored, separately —
they are different tasks with different error profiles, and merging them would
hide the most interesting question the ledger can ask.

Third-party testimony (somebody else asserting something) is recordable with
`--speaker`, and is excluded from the operator's own score by construction.

THE STANDING DIRECTIVE IS ENFORCED HERE, NOT REMEMBERED

`CLAUDE.md` carries a moratorium on new narrative about one living person. A
testimony record is new writing about the subject of the claim, and the
generated page is public, so the rule is mechanical and refuses rather than
warns: `record` will not take a claim that names her, and `check` fails if one
appears in the log or on the page. This BIASES THE SAMPLE — at least one
cleanly adjudicated confirmation is excluded by it — and the page says so
rather than quietly reporting a score drawn from a filtered record.

EVENT-SOURCED, IN PLAIN FILES

`testimony/events.jsonl` is the source of truth: append-only, one JSON object
per line, never rewritten. `testimony/testimonies.json` is a projection,
regenerated on every write and safe to delete. An adjudication that turns out
to be wrong is SUPERSEDED, never edited — the ledger shows the current verdict
and the log remembers that it changed, which matters more here than anywhere
else in this repository: a veracity record that can be quietly rewritten is
worth less than no veracity record at all.

Pure stdlib. Reads and appends `testimony/`, generates
`wiki/meta/testimony-veracity.md` and `testimony/SUMMARY.md`, shells to nothing.
"""
import argparse
import datetime
import json
import math
import re
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
LEDGER = ROOT / "testimony"
LOG = LEDGER / "events.jsonl"
PROJECTION = LEDGER / "testimonies.json"
SUMMARY = LEDGER / "SUMMARY.md"
PAGE = ROOT / "wiki" / "meta" / "testimony-veracity.md"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the directive
#
# Same pattern and the same reasoning as `bin/wiki-plain`: the rule is code
# rather than something a session has to remember, and it refuses rather than
# strips. See that file's header and CLAUDE.md's STANDING DIRECTIVE section.
MORATORIUM = re.compile(r"\b(annie|ulmer|lo_weez)\b", re.I)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- taxonomy
#
# CLAIM CLASSES are the "what". They are cut by the KIND OF PROPOSITION rather
# than by subject matter, because the error profile follows the proposition:
# a date is misremembered the same way whether it is about a job or a funeral,
# and lumping "claims about work" together would mix a date, a quantity and a
# motive into one meaningless rate.
CLAIM_CLASSES = {
    "date":        "when something happened — a day, month, season or year",
    "duration":    "how long something lasted, or how far apart two things were",
    "quantity":    "counts, sums, money, volumes, sizes",
    "sequence":    "the order of events, or which caused which to be scheduled",
    "identity":    "who somebody is — a name, a surname, a role, a handle",
    "attribution": "who said or did a particular thing",
    "existence":   "whether an event, object or document exists at all",
    "self_state":  "his own internal state, motive or reasoning at the time",
    "other_state": "somebody else's internal state, motive or intent",
    "causation":   "why something happened — one thing accounted for by another",
    "verbatim":    "the exact wording of something said or written",
    "enumeration": "a list presented as covering some set",
    "location":    "where somebody or something physically was",
    "behavior":    "his own habitual or aggregate conduct — 'I always', 'I never'",
}

# FAILURE MODES are the "how". Recorded only on a claim that did not come back
# clean, and a claim may carry several.
FAILURE_MODES = {
    "compression":   "the interval was remembered as shorter than it was",
    "expansion":     "the interval was remembered as longer than it was",
    "displacement":  "the whole thing sits at the wrong point on the timeline",
    "conflation":    "two separate events remembered as one",
    "transposition": "the facts are right and attached to the wrong person, place or time",
    "inversion":     "the direction is backwards — who did what to whom, or what caused what",
    "magnitude":     "right kind of thing, wrong size",
    "confabulation": "a detail that appears to have no referent at all",
    "omission":      "materially incomplete, presented as if complete",
    "overreach":     "a real observation generalised past what it supports",
    "rounding":      "snapped to a clean boundary — a new year, a round number",
    "stale":         "true when formed, silently false by the time it was asserted",
}

# DIRECTION is the signed half of the failure, and it is the half that makes a
# correction possible. Knowing he is wrong about dates is nearly useless;
# knowing he is EARLY is a subtractable bias.
DIRECTIONS = {
    "early":   "dated or placed before the truth",
    "late":    "dated or placed after the truth",
    "over":    "quantity stated above the truth",
    "under":   "quantity stated below the truth",
    "inward":  "responsibility placed on himself that the record puts elsewhere",
    "outward": "responsibility placed elsewhere that the record puts on him",
    "none":    "no meaningful direction to the error",
}

# The direction of an error RELATIVE TO HIS OWN INTEREST, which is a different
# axis from whether it is early or late and is the one most likely to expose a
# systematic bias rather than a memory limit. Recorded on every non-confirmed
# adjudication, including as `neutral`, so the absence of self-serving error is
# itself evidence rather than an untested assumption.
SLANTS = {
    "flattering": "the error, had it stood, would have reflected better on him",
    "condemning": "the error, had it stood, would have reflected worse on him",
    "neutral":    "the error carries no credit or discredit either way",
}

CONFIDENCES = {"certain": 0.95, "confident": 0.80, "hedged": 0.60, "unsure": 0.40}

# How the claim reached the record. This is a real predictor and not bookkeeping:
# a fact typed into a platform profile years ago and a fact given in answer to a
# direct question are not the same evidentiary object, and the first has no
# occasion for care.
CHANNELS = {
    "capture":              "a dated capture in raw/, given in answer to a wiki gap",
    "session":              "stated in an AI session and preserved in a dossier",
    "platform_self_report": "a field he filled in on a platform — profile, bio, form",
    "inline":               "quoted on a wiki page as his account, sourced to raw/",
    "message":              "asserted in a message to somebody at the time",
}

OUTCOMES = {
    "confirmed":        "independent evidence bears it out",
    "partial":          "the core holds, a material detail does not",
    "refuted":          "independent evidence contradicts it",
    "self_contradicted": "he has given two accounts that cannot both be true",
    "unfalsifiable":    "checked, and the corpus cannot settle it",
}

# What settled it. A confirmation by a primary document and a confirmation by
# something else he said are not the same event, and a ledger that cannot tell
# them apart will happily score him for agreeing with himself.
EVIDENCE_TIERS = {
    "primary_document":   "a dated document produced by a third party — a docket, a lease, a summons",
    "first_party_record": "a dated first-party artifact he did not write for the record — a message, a tweet, an export",
    "third_party":        "another person's account",
    "derived":            "a figure computed from the corpus",
    "none":               "nothing independent was found",
}

EVENT_TYPES = ("testimony_recorded", "testimony_adjudicated",
               "adjudication_revised", "testimony_voided")

# Outcome → the value the claim turned out to have. `partial` sits at the
# midpoint by construction and `self_contradicted` below it: two irreconcilable
# accounts mean at least one is wrong and nothing identifies which, which is
# worse evidentially than a half-right claim.
OUTCOME_VALUE = {"confirmed": 1.0, "partial": 0.5, "self_contradicted": 0.25, "refuted": 0.0}

# Below this, a class has no usable prior of its own and `assess` says so
# instead of quoting a rate computed from three cases.
MIN_N = 5
# Pseudocount for shrinking a class rate toward the global rate. Three is
# deliberately strong relative to the corpus size: at n=3 a class sits halfway
# between its own evidence and the global figure, which is where it belongs.
SHRINK_K = 3.0
# The floor under any bucket reported as a finding of its own -- a confidence
# band, a recall-span split. Below it the tool prints the count and withholds
# the interpretation, which is the rule `bin/intake` applies to a mean.
MIN_BUCKET = 3


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- small helpers
def today():
    return datetime.date.today().isoformat()


def wilson(successes, n, z=1.96):
    """Wilson score interval. A bare k/n reads as certainty it has not earned."""
    if n == 0:
        return (0.0, 1.0)
    p = successes / n
    d = 1 + z * z / n
    centre = (p + z * z / (2 * n)) / d
    half = z * math.sqrt(max(0.0, p * (1 - p) / n + z * z / (4 * n * n))) / d
    return (max(0.0, centre - half), min(1.0, centre + half))


def pct(x):
    return f"{100 * x:.0f}%"


def die(msg, code=2):
    print(f"wiki-testimony: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(code)


def moratorium_guard(*texts):
    """Refuse rather than strip. See the header."""
    for t in texts:
        if t and MORATORIUM.search(str(t)):
            hit = MORATORIUM.search(str(t)).group(0)
            die(f"refused — this names '{hit}', who is under the standing directive in "
                f"CLAUDE.md. The directive is a stop on new writing, and a testimony "
                f"record is new writing. Nothing was appended.")


def read_log():
    if not LOG.exists():
        return []
    out = []
    for i, line in enumerate(LOG.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines(), 1):
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            out.append(json.loads(line))
        except json.JSONDecodeError as exc:
            die(f"testimony/events.jsonl:{i} is not valid JSON ({exc}). "
                f"The log is the record — repair the line, never truncate the file.")
    return out


def append(event):
    LEDGER.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with LOG.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(json.dumps(event, ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True) + "\n")


def next_id(events):
    n = sum(1 for e in events if e.get("type") == "testimony_recorded")
    return f"t{n + 1:03d}"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the projection
def project(events):
    """Replay the log into current state. Pure; the log is never consulted again."""
    t = {}
    for e in events:
        et, tid = e.get("type"), e.get("id")
        if et == "testimony_recorded":
            rec = dict(e.get("testimony", {}))
            rec.update({"id": tid, "recorded_at": e.get("at"),
                        "adjudication": None, "history": []})
            t[tid] = rec
        elif et == "testimony_adjudicated":
            if tid in t:
                t[tid]["adjudication"] = dict(e.get("adjudication", {}))
        elif et == "adjudication_revised":
            if tid in t:
                prior = t[tid].get("adjudication")
                if prior:
                    t[tid]["history"].append(
                        {"superseded_at": e.get("at"),
                         "reason": e.get("reason", ""), "was": prior})
                t[tid]["adjudication"] = dict(e.get("adjudication", {}))
        elif et == "testimony_voided":
            if tid in t:
                t[tid]["voided"] = {"at": e.get("at"), "reason": e.get("reason", "")}
    live = {k: v for k, v in t.items() if not v.get("voided")}
    return {
        "generated": "bin/wiki-testimony — do not hand-edit; the log is the record",
        "counts": {
            "testimonies": len(live),
            "adjudicated": sum(1 for v in live.values() if v.get("adjudication")),
            "voided": sum(1 for v in t.values() if v.get("voided")),
        },
        "testimonies": {k: t[k] for k in sorted(t)},
    }


# The operator under any other name. `--speaker` exists so third-party
# testimony can be recorded and kept OUT of Dan's own score — correct, and the
# reason every statistic here filters on `speaker == "operator"`. The trap is
# that the filter is silent, so a first-person claim filed as `--speaker dan`
# is treated as somebody else's and disappears from the score, the profile and
# the public page with nothing saying so.
#
# It had already happened twice when this was written (2026-09-05): **t017**
# (*"As of May 2017 Dan was spending well over $100 a day on cocaine"*, filed
# `dan`) and **t020** (the $14,000 loan framing, filed `Dan`). The headline was
# being computed over 18 of 20 records and reporting no such thing.
SPEAKER_ALIASES = {"dan", "dan frank", "danfrank", "d", "self", "operator",
                   "dfrank88@gmail.com"}


def normalise_speaker(name):
    """`dan`, `Dan`, `Dan Frank` and `self` all mean the operator."""
    n = (name or "operator").strip()
    return "operator" if n.lower() in SPEAKER_ALIASES else n


def live_records(state, speaker="operator"):
    """Adjudicable records for one speaker. Voided rows never reach a statistic."""
    return [v for v in state["testimonies"].values()
            if not v.get("voided") and v.get("speaker", "operator") == speaker]


def weight(rec):
    """How much this claim is worth getting right.

    Specificity, because "sometime in the nineties" and "4 April 2018" are not
    the same assertion and must not move the score equally. Load-bearing, because
    a claim other pages reason FROM does more damage when it is wrong than one
    sitting on a single page with nothing downstream of it.
    """
    w = float(rec.get("specificity", 2))
    return w * (1.5 if rec.get("load_bearing") else 1.0)


def scored(records):
    """The subset that can move a score, with its outcome value attached.

    `unfalsifiable` and unadjudicated are excluded here rather than scored as
    zero, which is a real distinction: a zero would drag a mean toward the
    middle, while exclusion leaves the mean to the claims that were actually
    settled and lets the counts report the rest.
    """
    out = []
    for r in records:
        a = r.get("adjudication")
        if not a:
            continue
        v = OUTCOME_VALUE.get(a.get("outcome"))
        if v is None:
            continue
        out.append((r, a, v))
    return out


def veracity(records):
    """Points in, points out, and the 0-100 figure they make.

    points = weight x (2v - 1): a confirmed claim earns its full weight, a
    refuted one loses it, a partial is a wash. That is the plain reading of
    "given or lose points" and it is deliberately not the calibration term,
    which is a separate number computed below for a separate purpose.
    """
    rows = scored(records)
    if not rows:
        return {"n": 0, "score": None, "points": 0.0, "weight": 0.0, "rate": None}
    pts = sum(weight(r) * (2 * v - 1) for r, _, v in rows)
    wt = sum(weight(r) for r, _, v in rows)
    rate = sum(v for _, _, v in rows) / len(rows)
    return {"n": len(rows), "score": 50 + 50 * (pts / wt) if wt else None,
            "points": pts, "weight": wt, "rate": rate}


def calibration(records):
    """Brier skill against a coin flip, plus the over/under-confidence split.

    The skill score answers "is his confidence worth reading at all"; the split
    answers "which way does it fail", and only the second one tells you what to
    do about a claim that arrives marked `certain`.
    """
    rows = scored(records)
    if not rows:
        return {"n": 0, "brier": None, "skill": None, "bands": {}, "gap": None}
    briers, bands = [], {}
    for r, _, v in rows:
        p = CONFIDENCES.get(r.get("confidence", "confident"), 0.8)
        briers.append((p - v) ** 2)
        b = bands.setdefault(r.get("confidence", "confident"),
                             {"n": 0, "value": 0.0, "stated": p})
        b["n"] += 1
        b["value"] += v
    for b in bands.values():
        b["actual"] = b["value"] / b["n"]
        b["gap"] = b["actual"] - b["stated"]
    brier = sum(briers) / len(briers)
    stated = sum(CONFIDENCES.get(r.get("confidence", "confident"), 0.8) for r, _, _ in rows) / len(rows)
    actual = sum(v for _, _, v in rows) / len(rows)
    return {"n": len(rows), "brier": brier, "skill": 1 - brier / 0.25,
            "bands": bands, "gap": actual - stated}


def by_class(records):
    """Per-class veracity, with the shrunk rate `assess` actually uses."""
    rows = scored(records)
    glob = (sum(v for _, _, v in rows) / len(rows)) if rows else 0.5
    out = {}
    for r, a, v in rows:
        for c in r.get("classes", []) or ["unclassified"]:
            d = out.setdefault(c, {"n": 0, "value": 0.0, "confirmed": 0,
                                   "failures": {}, "directions": {}, "slants": {}})
            d["n"] += 1
            d["value"] += v
            if a.get("outcome") == "confirmed":
                d["confirmed"] += 1
            for f in a.get("failure", []):
                d["failures"][f] = d["failures"].get(f, 0) + 1
            dr = a.get("direction")
            if dr and dr != "none":
                d["directions"][dr] = d["directions"].get(dr, 0) + 1
            sl = a.get("slant")
            if sl:
                d["slants"][sl] = d["slants"].get(sl, 0) + 1
    for d in out.values():
        d["rate"] = d["value"] / d["n"]
        d["shrunk"] = (SHRINK_K * glob + d["value"]) / (SHRINK_K + d["n"])
        d["lo"], d["hi"] = wilson(d["confirmed"], d["n"])
    return out, glob


def top(counter, k=3):
    return sorted(counter.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))[:k]


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- commands
def cmd_record(args):
    """Enter a claim into the record. Does not adjudicate it."""
    moratorium_guard(args.claim, args.evidence_note, args.source)
    for c in args.claim_class:
        if c not in CLAIM_CLASSES:
            die(f"unknown claim class '{c}' — one of {', '.join(sorted(CLAIM_CLASSES))}")
    if args.channel not in CHANNELS:
        die(f"unknown channel '{args.channel}' — one of {', '.join(sorted(CHANNELS))}")
    if not 1 <= args.specificity <= 3:
        die("specificity is 1 (vague), 2 (definite) or 3 (exact)")
    events = read_log()
    tid = next_id(events)
    rec = {
        "claim": args.claim.strip(),
        "classes": list(args.claim_class),
        "confidence": args.confidence,
        "channel": args.channel,
        "specificity": args.specificity,
        "load_bearing": bool(args.load_bearing),
        "subject": args.subject,
        "speaker": normalise_speaker(args.speaker),
        "asserted": args.asserted or today(),
        "about": args.about or "",
        "source": args.source or "",
        "pages": list(args.page or []),
        "note": (args.evidence_note or "").strip(),
    }
    append({"type": "testimony_recorded", "id": tid, "at": today(), "testimony": rec})
    state = project(read_log())
    write_projection(state)
    print(f"recorded {tid} · {rec['claim'][:70]}")
    print(f"  classes {', '.join(rec['classes'])} · {rec['confidence']} · "
          f"specificity {rec['specificity']}"
          f"{' · load-bearing' if rec['load_bearing'] else ''}")
    print(f"  unadjudicated — it scores nothing until something settles it")
    return 0


def cmd_adjudicate(args):
    """Settle a claim against evidence, or record that the corpus cannot."""
    moratorium_guard(args.evidence)
    events = read_log()
    state = project(events)
    rec = state["testimonies"].get(args.id)
    if not rec:
        die(f"no testimony {args.id}")
    if rec.get("voided"):
        die(f"{args.id} was voided — reinstate it in the log before adjudicating")
    if rec.get("adjudication") and not args.revise:
        a = rec["adjudication"]
        die(f"{args.id} is already adjudicated as {a['outcome']} on {a.get('at')}. "
            f"Pass --revise with --reason to supersede it — an adjudication is "
            f"never edited, only superseded.")
    if args.revise and not rec.get("adjudication"):
        die(f"{args.id} has no adjudication to revise")
    if args.revise and not args.reason:
        die("--revise needs --reason: why the earlier verdict does not stand")
    if args.outcome not in OUTCOMES:
        die(f"unknown outcome — one of {', '.join(sorted(OUTCOMES))}")
    if args.tier not in EVIDENCE_TIERS:
        die(f"unknown evidence tier — one of {', '.join(sorted(EVIDENCE_TIERS))}")
    for f in args.failure:
        if f not in FAILURE_MODES:
            die(f"unknown failure mode '{f}' — one of {', '.join(sorted(FAILURE_MODES))}")
    if args.direction not in DIRECTIONS:
        die(f"unknown direction — one of {', '.join(sorted(DIRECTIONS))}")
    if args.slant and args.slant not in SLANTS:
        die(f"unknown slant — one of {', '.join(sorted(SLANTS))}")

    # A claim that did not come back clean has to say HOW it broke. Without
    # that this degenerates into a scoreboard, and a scoreboard predicts
    # nothing about the next claim.
    if args.outcome in ("refuted", "partial", "self_contradicted"):
        if not args.failure:
            die(f"outcome '{args.outcome}' requires at least one --failure — "
                f"the failure mode is what makes this ledger predictive rather "
                f"than merely a tally. One of: {', '.join(sorted(FAILURE_MODES))}")
        if not args.slant:
            die(f"outcome '{args.outcome}' requires --slant "
                f"({'/'.join(sorted(SLANTS))}) — whether an error runs toward "
                f"flattering him or condemning him is the single most diagnostic "
                f"column here, and `neutral` is a real answer that has to be given "
                f"rather than assumed.")
    if args.outcome == "confirmed" and args.tier == "none":
        die("a confirmation with evidence tier 'none' is not a confirmation — "
            "use 'unfalsifiable' if nothing independent was found")

    adj = {
        "outcome": args.outcome,
        "tier": args.tier,
        "evidence": (args.evidence or "").strip(),
        "failure": list(args.failure),
        "direction": args.direction,
        "magnitude": (args.magnitude or "").strip(),
        "slant": args.slant or "",
        "at": args.at or today(),
    }
    if args.revise:
        append({"type": "adjudication_revised", "id": args.id, "at": today(),
                "reason": args.reason, "adjudication": adj})
    else:
        append({"type": "testimony_adjudicated", "id": args.id, "at": today(),
                "adjudication": adj})
    state = project(read_log())
    write_projection(state)
    rec = state["testimonies"][args.id]
    v = OUTCOME_VALUE.get(args.outcome)
    print(f"{'revised' if args.revise else 'adjudicated'} {args.id} · {args.outcome}")
    if v is None:
        print("  scores nothing — the corpus could not settle it, which is not his failure")
    else:
        print(f"  {weight(rec) * (2 * v - 1):+.1f} points (weight {weight(rec):.1f})")
    return 0


def cmd_void(args):
    """Withdraw a record that should never have been one."""
    state = project(read_log())
    if args.id not in state["testimonies"]:
        die(f"no testimony {args.id}")
    if not args.reason:
        die("--reason is required: a voided record stays in the log and has to explain itself")
    append({"type": "testimony_voided", "id": args.id, "at": today(), "reason": args.reason})
    write_projection(project(read_log()))
    print(f"voided {args.id} — it stays in the log; nothing is deleted here")
    return 0


def cmd_list(args):
    state = project(read_log())
    rows = [v for v in state["testimonies"].values() if not v.get("voided")]
    if args.pending:
        rows = [r for r in rows if not r.get("adjudication")]
    if args.claim_class:
        rows = [r for r in rows if set(args.claim_class) & set(r.get("classes", []))]
    if args.outcome:
        rows = [r for r in rows
                if (r.get("adjudication") or {}).get("outcome") == args.outcome]
    if not rows:
        print("nothing matches.")
        return 0
    for r in sorted(rows, key=lambda x: x["id"]):
        a = r.get("adjudication")
        mark = {"confirmed": "✓", "refuted": "✗", "partial": "~",
                "self_contradicted": "±", "unfalsifiable": "?"}.get(
                    (a or {}).get("outcome"), "·")
        print(f"{mark} {r['id']}  {r['claim'][:78]}")
        print(f"    {', '.join(r.get('classes', []))} · {r.get('confidence')} · "
              f"{r.get('channel')} · about {r.get('about') or '—'}")
        if a:
            bits = [a["outcome"], a["tier"]]
            if a.get("failure"):
                bits.append("/".join(a["failure"]))
            if a.get("direction") and a["direction"] != "none":
                bits.append(a["direction"])
            if a.get("magnitude"):
                bits.append(a["magnitude"])
            print(f"    → {' · '.join(bits)}")
    print(f"\n{len(rows)} testimon{'y' if len(rows) == 1 else 'ies'}.")
    return 0


def cmd_show(args):
    state = project(read_log())
    r = state["testimonies"].get(args.id)
    if not r:
        die(f"no testimony {args.id}")
    print(f"{r['id']} — {r['claim']}\n")
    print(f"  classes      {', '.join(r.get('classes', []))}")
    print(f"  confidence   {r.get('confidence')} (stated p={CONFIDENCES.get(r.get('confidence'), 0.8)})")
    print(f"  channel      {r.get('channel')} — {CHANNELS.get(r.get('channel'), '')}")
    print(f"  specificity  {r.get('specificity')}   load-bearing: {r.get('load_bearing')}")
    print(f"  subject      {r.get('subject')}   speaker: {r.get('speaker')}")
    print(f"  asserted     {r.get('asserted')}   about: {r.get('about') or '—'}")
    print(f"  source       {r.get('source') or '—'}")
    if r.get("pages"):
        print(f"  pages        {', '.join(r['pages'])}")
    if r.get("note"):
        print(f"\n  {r['note']}")
    a = r.get("adjudication")
    if not a:
        print("\n  UNADJUDICATED — scores nothing, and is the queue rather than the record.")
    else:
        print(f"\n  OUTCOME      {a['outcome']} — {OUTCOMES[a['outcome']]}")
        print(f"  evidence     [{a['tier']}] {a.get('evidence') or '—'}")
        if a.get("failure"):
            for f in a["failure"]:
                print(f"  failure      {f} — {FAILURE_MODES[f]}")
        if a.get("direction") and a["direction"] != "none":
            print(f"  direction    {a['direction']} — {DIRECTIONS[a['direction']]}")
        if a.get("magnitude"):
            print(f"  magnitude    {a['magnitude']}")
        if a.get("slant"):
            print(f"  slant        {a['slant']} — {SLANTS[a['slant']]}")
        print(f"  adjudicated  {a.get('at')}")
    for h in r.get("history", []):
        print(f"\n  SUPERSEDED {h['superseded_at']}: was {h['was']['outcome']} — {h['reason']}")
    return 0


def cmd_score(args):
    """The trust score — and the second number that makes it usable."""
    state = project(read_log())
    recs = live_records(state, speaker=args.speaker)
    if args.subject:
        recs = [r for r in recs if r.get("subject") == args.subject]
    ver, cal = veracity(recs), calibration(recs)
    total = len(recs)
    pending = sum(1 for r in recs if not r.get("adjudication"))
    unfals = sum(1 for r in recs
                 if (r.get("adjudication") or {}).get("outcome") == "unfalsifiable")

    print(f"OPERATOR TESTIMONY — {args.speaker}"
          + (f", claims about {args.subject}" if args.subject else ""))
    print("=" * 62)
    if ver["n"] == 0:
        print(f"\n  No settled testimony yet. {total} recorded, {pending} awaiting "
              f"adjudication.\n  A trust score over nothing is not a low score, it is "
              f"an absent one.")
        return 0

    print(f"\n  VERACITY   {ver['score']:.0f} / 100        "
          f"n = {ver['n']} settled claims")
    print(f"             {ver['points']:+.1f} points on {ver['weight']:.1f} "
          f"weight · outcome value {ver['rate']:.2f}")
    lo, hi = wilson(sum(1 for _, a, _ in scored(recs) if a["outcome"] == "confirmed"), ver["n"])
    print(f"             clean-confirmation rate {pct(lo)}–{pct(hi)} (95% Wilson)")

    if cal["skill"] is not None:
        verdict = ("his confidence is worth reading" if cal["skill"] > 0.3 else
                   "his confidence carries some signal" if cal["skill"] > 0 else
                   "his confidence is not currently informative")
        if cal["n"] < MIN_N * 2:
            verdict += " — but at n=%d this is a reading, not a result" % cal["n"]
        print(f"\n  CALIBRATION  Brier {cal['brier']:.3f} · skill {cal['skill']:+.2f} "
              f"vs a coin flip")
        print(f"             {verdict}")
        g = cal["gap"]
        word = "over-confident" if g < -0.02 else "under-confident" if g > 0.02 else "well-matched"
        print(f"             stated vs actual gap {g:+.2f} — {word}")
        if cal["bands"]:
            print(f"\n  {'band':<11}{'says':>6}{'is':>7}{'gap':>8}{'n':>5}")
            for b in ("certain", "confident", "hedged", "unsure"):
                d = cal["bands"].get(b)
                if not d:
                    continue
                thin = "  ·" if d["n"] < MIN_BUCKET else ""
                print(f"  {b:<11}{d['stated']:>6.2f}{d['actual']:>7.2f}"
                      f"{d['gap']:>+8.2f}{d['n']:>5}{thin}")
            print(f"  · = fewer than {MIN_BUCKET} settled claims; that gap is "
                  f"not yet a measurement")

    print(f"\n  Not scored: {pending} unadjudicated, {unfals} unfalsifiable "
          f"(checked, corpus silent — scores zero, never negative).")

    cls, glob = by_class(recs)
    if cls:
        print(f"\n  BY CLASS — shrunk toward the global {glob:.2f} with k={SHRINK_K:g}; "
              f"a class under n={MIN_N} is not a prior")
        print(f"  {'class':<14}{'n':>3}{'raw':>7}{'shrunk':>8}   dominant failure")
        for c, d in sorted(cls.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1]["n"], kv[0])):
            fails = top(d["failures"], 2)
            fs = ", ".join(f"{k} x{v}" for k, v in fails) if fails else "—"
            flag = "" if d["n"] >= MIN_N else "  ·"
            print(f"  {c:<14}{d['n']:>3}{d['rate']:>7.2f}{d['shrunk']:>8.2f}   {fs}{flag}")
    return 0


def cmd_profile(args):
    """The 'what and how' — the shape of the error, not its size."""
    state = project(read_log())
    recs = live_records(state, speaker=args.speaker)
    rows = scored(recs)
    if not rows:
        print("Nothing settled yet — there is no shape to report.")
        return 0

    print("HOW THE ERRORS RUN")
    print("=" * 62)

    fails, dirs, slants, tiers, chans = {}, {}, {}, {}, {}
    for r, a, v in rows:
        for f in a.get("failure", []):
            fails[f] = fails.get(f, 0) + 1
        d = a.get("direction")
        if d and d != "none":
            dirs[d] = dirs.get(d, 0) + 1
        if a.get("slant"):
            slants[a["slant"]] = slants.get(a["slant"], 0) + 1
        tiers[a["tier"]] = tiers.get(a["tier"], 0) + 1
        ch = r.get("channel", "?")
        c = chans.setdefault(ch, {"n": 0, "value": 0.0})
        c["n"] += 1
        c["value"] += v

    wrong = [(r, a, v) for r, a, v in rows if v < 1.0]
    print(f"\n  {len(wrong)} of {len(rows)} settled claims came back less than clean.")

    if fails:
        print("\n  FAILURE MODES")
        for k, n in top(fails, 99):
            print(f"    {n:>2}x  {k:<14} {FAILURE_MODES[k]}")
    if dirs:
        print("\n  DIRECTION — the subtractable half. A bias you can name is a bias")
        print("  you can correct for; an error rate is only a warning.")
        for k, n in top(dirs, 99):
            print(f"    {n:>2}x  {k:<14} {DIRECTIONS[k]}")
    if slants:
        print("\n  SLANT — which way the error runs relative to his own interest")
        tot = sum(slants.values())
        for k, n in top(slants, 99):
            print(f"    {n:>2}x  ({pct(n / tot):>4})  {k:<11} {SLANTS[k]}")
        f, c = slants.get("flattering", 0), slants.get("condemning", 0)
        if f or c:
            if c > f:
                print("\n    The errors run AGAINST him more often than for him. An"
                      "\n    unreliable narrator whose distortions are self-condemning is"
                      "\n    a different object from one whose distortions are"
                      "\n    self-flattering, and should be discounted differently: the"
                      "\n    claims to re-check hardest are the ones where he is the"
                      "\n    accused.")
            elif f > c:
                print("\n    The errors run FOR him more often than against. Weigh"
                      "\n    self-exculpating detail below the rest of the record.")
            else:
                print("\n    Errors split evenly for and against him — no directional"
                      "\n    self-interest visible at this n.")

    if chans:
        print("\n  BY CHANNEL — how the claim reached the record")
        for k, d in sorted(chans.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["n"]):
            print(f"    {d['n']:>2}  {k:<22} outcome value {d['value'] / d['n']:.2f}"
                  f"   {CHANNELS.get(k, '')}")
    if tiers:
        print("\n  WHAT SETTLED THEM")
        for k, n in top(tiers, 99):
            print(f"    {n:>2}x  {k:<20} {EVIDENCE_TIERS[k]}")

    span = []
    for r, a, v in rows:
        ab, asrt = r.get("about", ""), r.get("asserted", "")
        m1, m2 = re.match(r"(\d{4})", ab or ""), re.match(r"(\d{4})", asrt or "")
        if m1 and m2:
            span.append((int(m2.group(1)) - int(m1.group(1)), v))
    if len(span) >= 2 * MIN_BUCKET:
        near = [v for d, v in span if d <= 5]
        far = [v for d, v in span if d > 5]
        # Both buckets have to stand on their own. A 0.00 over one claim is not
        # a finding about recall span, it is one claim.
        if len(near) >= MIN_BUCKET and len(far) >= MIN_BUCKET:
            print("\n  RECALL SPAN — years between the event and the telling")
            print(f"    within 5 years   {sum(near) / len(near):.2f}  (n={len(near)})")
            print(f"    beyond 5 years   {sum(far) / len(far):.2f}  (n={len(far)})")
    return 0


def cmd_assess(args):
    """The point of the whole thing: a prior for a claim nobody can check yet."""
    state = project(read_log())
    recs = live_records(state, speaker="operator")
    cls, glob = by_class(recs)
    ver, cal = veracity(recs), calibration(recs)
    if ver["n"] == 0:
        print("No settled testimony — there is no prior to give. Record and "
              "adjudicate some claims first.")
        return 0

    c = args.claim_class
    if c and c not in CLAIM_CLASSES:
        die(f"unknown claim class '{c}' — one of {', '.join(sorted(CLAIM_CLASSES))}")
    conf = args.confidence

    print("ASSESSMENT OF AN UNPROVEN CLAIM")
    print("=" * 62)
    if args.claim:
        print(f"\n  \"{args.claim.strip()}\"")
    print(f"\n  class {c or '(unspecified)'} · asserted {conf or '(unstated)'}")

    d = cls.get(c) if c else None
    if d and d["n"] >= MIN_N:
        base, why = d["shrunk"], f"class rate, n={d['n']}, shrunk toward global {glob:.2f}"
        lo, hi = d["lo"], d["hi"]
    elif d:
        base = d["shrunk"]
        why = (f"class has only n={d['n']} — under MIN_N={MIN_N}, so this is mostly "
               f"the global rate ({glob:.2f}) and not a class finding")
        lo, hi = d["lo"], d["hi"]
    else:
        base, why = glob, f"no class evidence at all — global rate over n={ver['n']}"
        lo, hi = wilson(sum(1 for _, a, _ in scored(recs) if a["outcome"] == "confirmed"), ver["n"])

    print(f"\n  PRIOR        {base:.2f}   ({why})")
    print(f"               confirmation interval {pct(lo)}–{pct(hi)} (95% Wilson)")

    if conf:
        p = CONFIDENCES[conf]
        band = cal["bands"].get(conf)
        if band and band["n"] >= 3:
            print(f"\n  HIS '{conf}' has historically been worth {band['actual']:.2f}, "
                  f"not {p:.2f} (n={band['n']}).")
            adj = base + (band["gap"] * 0.5)
            print(f"  ADJUSTED     {max(0.0, min(1.0, adj)):.2f}   "
                  f"class prior moved halfway by the band's observed gap")
        else:
            print(f"\n  He states {p:.2f} for '{conf}'. Too few settled claims in that "
                  f"band (n={band['n'] if band else 0}) to say what it is really worth.")

    if d and d["n"]:
        f = top(d["failures"], 2)
        if f:
            print(f"\n  IF IT IS WRONG, expect: "
                  + "; ".join(f"{k} ({FAILURE_MODES[k]})" for k, _ in f))
        dr = top(d["directions"], 1)
        if dr:
            print(f"  DIRECTION    historically {dr[0][0]} — "
                  f"{DIRECTIONS[dr[0][0]]}. Correct in that direction before "
                  f"treating the figure as central.")
        sl = top(d["slants"], 1)
        if sl:
            print(f"  SLANT        errors in this class have run {sl[0][0]}.")

    print("\n  WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT")
    print("    A prior is not a verdict. Record the claim with `record`, name the "
          "\n    artifact that would decide it, and adjudicate when it lands. An "
          "\n    assessment that never becomes an adjudication teaches this ledger "
          "\n    nothing.")
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- derived files
def write_projection(state):
    """Both derived files, always together.

    They are generated from the same replay and `check` gates on both, so
    writing one without the other leaves the gate red for no reason a reader
    could act on.
    """
    LEDGER.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    PROJECTION.write_text(
        json.dumps(state, indent=2, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n",
        encoding="utf-8")
    SUMMARY.write_text(render_summary(state), encoding="utf-8")


def render_summary(state):
    """testimony/SUMMARY.md — the analysis surface, so nothing has to parse JSONL."""
    recs = live_records(state)
    ver, cal = veracity(recs), calibration(recs)
    cls, glob = by_class(recs)
    L = ["# Testimony ledger — summary", "",
         "_Generated by `bin/wiki-testimony summary`. Do not hand-edit; "
         "`testimony/events.jsonl` is the record._", ""]
    L.append(f"- **Recorded:** {len(recs)}")
    L.append(f"- **Settled (scoring):** {ver['n']}")
    L.append(f"- **Awaiting adjudication:** "
             f"{sum(1 for r in recs if not r.get('adjudication'))}")
    L.append(f"- **Unfalsifiable:** "
             f"{sum(1 for r in recs if (r.get('adjudication') or {}).get('outcome') == 'unfalsifiable')}"
             f" — checked, corpus silent, scores zero")
    L.append("")
    if ver["n"]:
        L += [f"**Veracity {ver['score']:.0f}/100** over n={ver['n']} "
              f"({ver['points']:+.1f} points on {ver['weight']:.1f} weight).",
              ""]
        if cal["skill"] is not None:
            L += [f"**Calibration** Brier {cal['brier']:.3f}, skill "
                  f"{cal['skill']:+.2f} against a coin flip; stated-vs-actual gap "
                  f"{cal['gap']:+.2f}.", ""]
        L += ["| class | n | raw | shrunk | dominant failure |", "|---|---:|---:|---:|---|"]
        for c, d in sorted(cls.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1]["n"], kv[0])):
            f = top(d["failures"], 2)
            L.append(f"| {c} | {d['n']} | {d['rate']:.2f} | {d['shrunk']:.2f} | "
                     f"{', '.join(k for k, _ in f) or '—'} |")
        L.append("")
        L.append(f"Read every rate with its `n`. Classes below n={MIN_N} are not "
                 f"priors and `assess` refuses to treat them as such.")
    else:
        L.append("Nothing settled yet.")
    L.append("")
    return "\n".join(L)


def render_page(state):
    """wiki/meta/testimony-veracity.md — the public face, evidence not claim.

    Generated, so a hand-edit fails `check`. Like the intake ledger this page
    states no finding of its own: a finding drawn from the ledger reaches an
    ordinary page through the normal operations, never through this one, which
    is overwritten on every write.
    """
    recs = live_records(state)
    ver, cal = veracity(recs), calibration(recs)
    cls, glob = by_class(recs)
    rows = scored(recs)
    pending = [r for r in recs if not r.get("adjudication")]
    unfals = [r for r in recs
              if (r.get("adjudication") or {}).get("outcome") == "unfalsifiable"]

    counts = {}
    for _, a, _ in rows:
        counts[a["outcome"]] = counts.get(a["outcome"], 0) + 1

    bands = cal.get("bands") or {}
    L = ["---",
         "domain: meta",
         "page_type: dataset",
         'title: "Operator Testimony Veracity"',
         'aliases: ["testimony ledger", "trust score", "veracity ledger"]',
         "status: active",
         "importance: high",
         "knowledge: derived",
         "date_created: 2026-09-02",
         f"date_modified: {today()}",
         "chart:",
         "  kind: grouped-bar",
         '  title: "Stated confidence against measured outcome, by confidence band"',
         '  x: { label: "Confidence band as asserted", type: category }',
         '  y: { label: "Probability the claim held up", type: number }',
         "  series:",
         '    - name: "What he said it was worth"',
         "      points:"]
    order = [b for b in ("certain", "confident", "hedged", "unsure") if b in bands]
    if not order:
        L.append('        "no data": 0')
    for b in order:
        L.append(f'        "{b}": {bands[b]["stated"]:.2f}')
    L.append('    - name: "What it turned out to be worth"')
    L.append("      points:")
    if not order:
        L.append('        "no data": 0')
    for b in order:
        L.append(f'        "{b}": {bands[b]["actual"]:.2f}')
    L += ["sources:",
          "  - testimony/events.jsonl",
          "connections: []",
          "related:",
          "  - wiki/meta/index",
          "  - wiki/self/concepts/wiki-brain",
          "  - wiki/health/intake-ledger",
          "---",
          "",
          "# Operator Testimony Veracity",
          "",
          "**This page is evidence, not a claim.** It is the standing state of "
          "`testimony/events.jsonl` — every first-person assertion the wiki has "
          "recorded, what settled it, and the arithmetic over the settled ones. "
          "It states no finding. A finding drawn from this ledger reaches an "
          "ordinary page through the normal operations; this one is regenerated "
          "by `bin/wiki-testimony page` and overwritten on every write.",
          ""]

    if ver["n"] == 0:
        L += ["No testimony has been settled yet. A trust score over nothing is "
              "not a low score — it is an absent one, and this page will say so "
              "until there is something to report.", ""]
    else:
        L += ["## The two numbers", "",
              "A single trust score would collapse two facts that behave "
              "differently. **Veracity** is how often he turns out to be right. "
              "**Calibration** is whether his own stated confidence tracks that. "
              "The first says how much to believe him; only the second makes an "
              "*unproven* claim assessable, because an unproven claim offers "
              "nothing to check except the class it belongs to and the "
              "confidence it arrived with.", "",
              "| | value | n |", "|---|---|---:|",
              f"| **Veracity** | **{ver['score']:.0f} / 100** — "
              f"{ver['points']:+.1f} points on {ver['weight']:.1f} weight | {ver['n']} |"]
        if cal["skill"] is not None:
            L.append(f"| **Calibration** | Brier {cal['brier']:.3f}, skill "
                     f"{cal['skill']:+.2f} vs a coin flip | {cal['n']} |")
            L.append(f"| **Stated vs actual** | {cal['gap']:+.2f} | {cal['n']} |")
        L.append("")
        L += [f"Points are `weight x (2v - 1)`: a confirmed claim earns its full "
              f"weight, a refuted one loses it, a partial is a wash. Weight is "
              f"specificity (1-3) times 1.5 where other pages reason from the "
              f"claim. **Read every figure with its n** — this ledger holds "
              f"{ver['n']} settled claims, not thousands.", ""]

        if bands:
            L += ["## What his confidence is actually worth", "",
                  "| band | he states | it is worth | gap | n |",
                  "|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|"]
            for b in order:
                d = bands[b]
                L.append(f"| {b} | {d['stated']:.2f} | {d['actual']:.2f} | "
                         f"{d['gap']:+.2f} | {d['n']} |")
            L.append("")

        L += ["## Outcomes", "", "| outcome | n | meaning |", "|---|---:|---|"]
        for o in ("confirmed", "partial", "self_contradicted", "refuted"):
            if counts.get(o):
                L.append(f"| {o} | {counts[o]} | {OUTCOMES[o]} |")
        if unfals:
            L.append(f"| unfalsifiable | {len(unfals)} | {OUTCOMES['unfalsifiable']} "
                     f"— **scores zero, never negative** |")
        if pending:
            L.append(f"| _unadjudicated_ | {len(pending)} | not yet checked; "
                     f"excluded from every statistic |")
        L.append("")

        if cls:
            L += ["## By claim class", "",
                  f"Rates are shrunk toward the global {glob:.2f} with a "
                  f"pseudocount of {SHRINK_K:g}, and a class below n={MIN_N} is "
                  f"not treated as a prior at all.", "",
                  "| class | n | raw | shrunk | 95% CI on clean confirmations | dominant failure |",
                  "|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---|"]
            for c, d in sorted(cls.items(), key=lambda kv: (-kv[1]["n"], kv[0])):
                f = top(d["failures"], 2)
                L.append(f"| {c} | {d['n']} | {d['rate']:.2f} | {d['shrunk']:.2f} | "
                         f"{pct(d['lo'])}–{pct(d['hi'])} | "
                         f"{', '.join(k for k, _ in f) or '—'} |")
            L.append("")

        slants = {}
        for _, a, _ in rows:
            if a.get("slant"):
                slants[a["slant"]] = slants.get(a["slant"], 0) + 1
        if slants:
            L += ["## Which way the errors run", "",
                  "Whether an error would have flattered him or condemned him, "
                  "had it stood. This is a different axis from early/late or "
                  "over/under, and it is the one that separates a memory limit "
                  "from a bias.", "",
                  "| slant | n |", "|---|---:|"]
            for k, n in top(slants, 99):
                L.append(f"| {k} — {SLANTS[k]} | {n} |")
            L.append("")

    L += ["## The record", "", "| id | claim | class | said | outcome | what settled it |",
          "|---|---|---|---|---|---|"]
    for r in sorted(recs, key=lambda x: x["id"]):
        a = r.get("adjudication") or {}
        out = a.get("outcome", "_pending_")
        ev = a.get("evidence", "") or "—"
        claim = r["claim"].replace("|", "\\|")
        ev = ev.replace("|", "\\|")
        L.append(f"| {r['id']} | {claim} | {', '.join(r.get('classes', []))} | "
                 f"{r.get('confidence', '')} | {out} | {ev} |")
    L.append("")

    L += ["## What this ledger cannot see", "",
          "**The sample is filtered, and not at random.** `CLAUDE.md` carries a "
          "standing directive stopping new writing about one living person. "
          "`bin/wiki-testimony` enforces it mechanically rather than leaving it "
          "to a session to remember, and refuses such a record outright. At "
          "least one cleanly adjudicated confirmation is excluded by that rule. "
          "The exclusion is correct and it is still a bias: this score is drawn "
          "from what the directive permits, not from everything the corpus knows.",
          "",
          "**Adjudicated claims are not a random sample of claims.** A claim gets "
          "checked when somebody had a reason to check it, and the reasons "
          "correlate with the claim being surprising, load-bearing, or already "
          "doubted. Expect the settled set to over-represent both the "
          "spectacular confirmations and the spectacular failures.",
          "",
          "**Nothing here measures honesty.** Every outcome in this ledger is "
          "consistent with a person reporting in good faith and misremembering. "
          "The failure-mode column exists precisely so the two can be told apart "
          "in principle — a distribution dominated by `compression` and "
          "`rounding` is a memory, one dominated by `confabulation` running "
          "`flattering` would be something else — and at this n it can be told "
          "apart in principle only.",
          "",
          "---",
          "",
          "Generated by `bin/wiki-testimony page` from `testimony/events.jsonl`. "
          "Do not hand-edit — rerun the tool.",
          "",
          "[[wiki/meta/index|meta]] · [[wiki/health/intake-ledger|Intake ledger]] · "
          "[[wiki/self/concepts/wiki-brain|The wiki]]",
          ""]
    return "\n".join(L)


def cmd_page(args):
    state = project(read_log())
    page = render_page(state)
    moratorium_guard(page)
    PAGE.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    PAGE.write_text(page, encoding="utf-8")
    write_projection(state)
    print(f"wrote {PAGE.relative_to(ROOT)}, {SUMMARY.relative_to(ROOT)} "
          f"and {PROJECTION.relative_to(ROOT)}")
    return 0


def cmd_rebuild(args):
    state = project(read_log())
    write_projection(state)
    print(f"rebuilt the projection from {len(read_log())} log events "
          f"— {state['counts']['testimonies']} testimonies")
    return 0


def cmd_check(args):
    """The gate. Silent and clean when nothing has ever been recorded."""
    errors, warnings = [], []
    events = read_log()
    if not events and not PAGE.exists():
        print("wiki-testimony: 0 errors, 0 warnings · ledger empty")
        return 0

    state = project(events)

    seen = set()
    for e in events:
        if e.get("type") not in EVENT_TYPES:
            errors.append(f"unknown event type '{e.get('type')}' in the log")
        if e.get("type") == "testimony_recorded":
            if e.get("id") in seen:
                errors.append(f"duplicate testimony id {e.get('id')} in the log")
            seen.add(e.get("id"))
        elif e.get("id") and e.get("id") not in seen:
            errors.append(f"{e.get('type')} references unknown testimony {e.get('id')}")

    for tid, r in state["testimonies"].items():
        if r.get("voided"):
            continue
        for c in r.get("classes", []):
            if c not in CLAIM_CLASSES:
                errors.append(f"{tid}: unknown claim class '{c}'")
        if r.get("confidence") not in CONFIDENCES:
            errors.append(f"{tid}: unknown confidence '{r.get('confidence')}'")
        # A record filed under any speaker but the operator is excluded from
        # every statistic and from the public page BY CONSTRUCTION — which is
        # right for real third-party testimony and silent when it is a
        # misfiling. Say it out loud on every run, so a deliberate third-party
        # record reads as deliberate and an accident is caught the next time
        # the gate runs. `record` now normalises the operator's own aliases;
        # this catches what is already in the log and anything a hand-written
        # event puts there.
        sp = r.get("speaker", "operator")
        if sp != "operator":
            warnings.append(
                f"{tid}: speaker '{sp}' — excluded from the score, the profile "
                f"and the public page. Intended for third-party testimony; if "
                f"this is Dan's own claim it is invisible and should be voided "
                f"and re-recorded")
        a = r.get("adjudication")
        if not a:
            continue
        if a.get("outcome") not in OUTCOMES:
            errors.append(f"{tid}: unknown outcome '{a.get('outcome')}'")
        if a.get("tier") not in EVIDENCE_TIERS:
            errors.append(f"{tid}: unknown evidence tier '{a.get('tier')}'")
        # The rule that keeps this a predictive instrument rather than a tally.
        if a.get("outcome") in ("refuted", "partial", "self_contradicted"):
            if not a.get("failure"):
                errors.append(f"{tid}: {a['outcome']} with no failure mode — "
                              f"the ledger cannot learn from an unexplained miss")
            if not a.get("slant"):
                errors.append(f"{tid}: {a['outcome']} with no slant recorded")
        if a.get("outcome") == "confirmed" and a.get("tier") == "none":
            errors.append(f"{tid}: confirmed on evidence tier 'none' — that is "
                          f"'unfalsifiable', not a confirmation")
        # A source is what makes this testimony rather than assertion. Warn
        # rather than fail: some genuinely arrive inline on a page.
        if not r.get("source"):
            warnings.append(f"{tid}: no source — where did he say this?")

    blob = json.dumps(state, ensure_ascii=False)
    if MORATORIUM.search(blob):
        errors.append("the ledger contains a record naming a person under the "
                      "standing directive in CLAUDE.md — this is published; "
                      "void the record rather than editing the log in place")

    fresh = json.dumps(state, indent=2, sort_keys=True, ensure_ascii=False) + "\n"
    if not PROJECTION.exists():
        errors.append("testimony/testimonies.json is missing — run `bin/wiki-testimony rebuild`")
    elif PROJECTION.read_text(encoding="utf-8") != fresh:
        errors.append("testimony/testimonies.json disagrees with the log — "
                      "run `bin/wiki-testimony rebuild` (the log is the record)")

    page = render_page(state)
    if not PAGE.exists():
        errors.append(f"{PAGE.relative_to(ROOT)} is missing — run `bin/wiki-testimony page`")
    elif PAGE.read_text(encoding="utf-8") != page:
        errors.append(f"{PAGE.relative_to(ROOT)} is behind the ledger — "
                      f"run `bin/wiki-testimony page` and do not hand-edit it")
    if MORATORIUM.search(page):
        errors.append("the generated page names a person under the standing directive")

    summ = render_summary(state)
    if SUMMARY.exists() and SUMMARY.read_text(encoding="utf-8") != summ:
        errors.append("testimony/SUMMARY.md is behind the ledger — "
                      "run `bin/wiki-testimony page`")

    for w in warnings:
        print(f"WARN  {w}")
    for e in errors:
        print(f"ERROR {e}")
    ver = veracity(live_records(state))
    print(f"wiki-testimony: {len(errors)} error{'s' if len(errors) != 1 else ''}, "
          f"{len(warnings)} warning{'s' if len(warnings) != 1 else ''} · "
          f"{state['counts']['testimonies']} testimonies, "
          f"{ver['n']} settled"
          + (f", veracity {ver['score']:.0f}/100" if ver["score"] is not None else ""))
    return 1 if errors else 0


def cmd_status(args):
    state = project(read_log())
    recs = live_records(state)
    if not recs:
        print("No testimony recorded yet.\n\n"
              "  bin/wiki-testimony record --claim \"...\" --class date "
              "--confidence certain \\\n"
              "      --channel capture --source raw/... --about 2019-06")
        return 0
    ver = veracity(recs)
    pending = [r for r in recs if not r.get("adjudication")]
    print(f"{len(recs)} testimonies · {ver['n']} settled · {len(pending)} awaiting adjudication")
    if ver["score"] is not None:
        print(f"veracity {ver['score']:.0f}/100 over n={ver['n']}")
    if pending:
        print("\nAwaiting adjudication — each is a claim the wiki is currently "
              "carrying on his word alone:")
        for r in sorted(pending, key=lambda x: x["id"])[:12]:
            print(f"  {r['id']}  {r['claim'][:72]}")
        if len(pending) > 12:
            print(f"  … and {len(pending) - 12} more")
    return 0


def cmd_taxonomy(args):
    for title, table in (("CLAIM CLASSES — the 'what'", CLAIM_CLASSES),
                         ("FAILURE MODES — the 'how'", FAILURE_MODES),
                         ("DIRECTIONS — the subtractable half", DIRECTIONS),
                         ("SLANTS — relative to his own interest", SLANTS),
                         ("CHANNELS — how the claim reached the record", CHANNELS),
                         ("OUTCOMES", OUTCOMES),
                         ("EVIDENCE TIERS", EVIDENCE_TIERS)):
        print(f"\n{title}")
        for k, v in table.items():
            print(f"  {k:<22} {v}")
    print(f"\nCONFIDENCE BANDS")
    for k, v in CONFIDENCES.items():
        print(f"  {k:<22} stated probability {v}")
    return 0


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="wiki-testimony",
        description="The operator testimony veracity ledger.")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd")

    p = sub.add_parser("record", help="enter a claim into the record")
    p.add_argument("--claim", required=True, help="the proposition, one falsifiable sentence")
    p.add_argument("--class", dest="claim_class", action="append", required=True,
                   metavar="CLASS", help="claim class (repeatable)")
    p.add_argument("--confidence", choices=sorted(CONFIDENCES), default="confident")
    p.add_argument("--channel", default="capture", help="how it reached the record")
    p.add_argument("--specificity", type=int, default=2,
                   help="1 vague, 2 definite, 3 exact")
    p.add_argument("--load-bearing", action="store_true",
                   help="other pages reason from this claim")
    p.add_argument("--subject", choices=("self", "other"), default="self")
    p.add_argument("--speaker", default="operator")
    p.add_argument("--asserted", help="date he said it (default today)")
    p.add_argument("--about", help="date or period the claim is ABOUT")
    p.add_argument("--source", help="raw/ path or wiki page recording the assertion")
    p.add_argument("--page", action="append", help="wiki page reasoning from it (repeatable)")
    p.add_argument("--note", dest="evidence_note", help="context, and what would settle it")
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_record)

    p = sub.add_parser("adjudicate", help="settle a claim against evidence")
    p.add_argument("id")
    p.add_argument("--outcome", required=True, choices=sorted(OUTCOMES))
    p.add_argument("--tier", required=True, choices=sorted(EVIDENCE_TIERS),
                   help="what class of evidence settled it")
    p.add_argument("--evidence", help="the citation that settled it")
    p.add_argument("--failure", action="append", default=[],
                   help="failure mode (repeatable; required unless confirmed)")
    p.add_argument("--direction", default="none", choices=sorted(DIRECTIONS))
    p.add_argument("--magnitude", help="how far off, in its own units")
    p.add_argument("--slant", choices=sorted(SLANTS),
                   help="which way the error ran relative to his interest")
    p.add_argument("--at", help="date of adjudication (default today)")
    p.add_argument("--revise", action="store_true", help="supersede an existing verdict")
    p.add_argument("--reason", help="why the earlier verdict does not stand")
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_adjudicate)

    p = sub.add_parser("void", help="withdraw a record that should not be one")
    p.add_argument("id")
    p.add_argument("--reason", required=True)
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_void)

    p = sub.add_parser("list", help="the record")
    p.add_argument("--pending", action="store_true", help="only unadjudicated")
    p.add_argument("--class", dest="claim_class", action="append")
    p.add_argument("--outcome", choices=sorted(OUTCOMES))
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_list)

    p = sub.add_parser("show", help="one testimony in full")
    p.add_argument("id")
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_show)

    p = sub.add_parser("score", help="the trust score, global and by class")
    p.add_argument("--speaker", default="operator")
    p.add_argument("--subject", choices=("self", "other"))
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_score)

    p = sub.add_parser("profile", help="what and how he gets things wrong")
    p.add_argument("--speaker", default="operator")
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_profile)

    p = sub.add_parser("assess", help="a prior for an unproven claim")
    p.add_argument("--class", dest="claim_class")
    p.add_argument("--confidence", choices=sorted(CONFIDENCES))
    p.add_argument("--claim", help="the claim text, for the printout")
    p.set_defaults(fn=cmd_assess)

    for name, fn, helptext in (
            ("page", cmd_page, "regenerate the wiki page, summary and projection"),
            ("summary", cmd_page, "alias for page"),
            ("rebuild", cmd_rebuild, "regenerate the projection from the log"),
            ("check", cmd_check, "the gate"),
            ("status", cmd_status, "the standing state"),
            ("taxonomy", cmd_taxonomy, "every class, mode, direction and tier")):
        q = sub.add_parser(name, help=helptext)
        q.set_defaults(fn=fn)

    args = ap.parse_args()
    if not args.cmd:
        return cmd_status(args)
    return args.fn(args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
